   "response_text": "Now let me analyze what I've found:\n\n**Key Observations:**\n\n1. **Configuration Parameters:**\n   - Learning rate: 0.01\n   - Batch size: 256\n   - Epochs: 20\n   - Optimizer: Adam\n   - Weight decay: 0.0001\n   - Label noise fraction: 0.38 (38% of training labels are flipped)\n\n2. **Training Metrics:**\n   - Train loss gradually decreases from 0.6807 to 0.6711 (about 1.4% reduction over 20 epochs)\n   - Validation accuracy fluctuates between ~0.805 and ~0.842, mostly in the 0.83 range\n   - The training curve is very flat - loss decreases very slowly\n   - Validation accuracy shows high volatility with no clear improving trend\n\n3. **Concerning Pattern:**\n   - The loss barely moves: only drops ~0.009 over 20 epochs\n   - Validation accuracy is noisy and doesn't show improvement - actually it's worse at the end (0.8266) than early on (0.8412)\n   - A 38% label noise corruption is extremely high - this would make it very difficult for the model to learn\n\nLet me check if the label noise is being applied correctly by looking at the interaction:\n\nThe issue I see is the extremely high label noise fraction (0.38 = 38%). With 38% of training labels randomly flipped, the model faces a contradictory signal that makes learning nearly impossible. This is unreasonably high and appears to be a configuration error.\n\nFor reference:\n- A 0.38 label noise fraction means nearly 2 out of 5 labels are randomly inverted\n- This severely limits what any model can learn from the training data\n- The near-flat loss curve and non-improving validation accuracy are consistent with this corruption level\n\nLet me verify by looking at typical label noise values for this dataset. The problem is that such high label noise is atypical and problematic for normal training. Let me also check if there's a reference configuration:",
