   "turn": 8,
   "response_text": "Based on my investigation, I've identified the incident:\n\n**Diagnosis:**\nThe training run has an **excessive label noise configuration** set to 0.38 (38%). This causes:\n- Near-flat training loss curve (decreases only ~0.009 over 20 epochs)\n- Non-improving validation accuracy that actually degrades after epoch 1\n- The model cannot effectively learn from training data with 38% of labels randomly flipped\n\nA label noise fraction of 0.38 is extremely high and unrealistic for a standard training scenario. Typical label noise in robustness studies is usually 0.05-0.20 (5-20%), and 0.38 creates such conflicting signals that meaningful learning becomes nearly impossible.\n\nThe evidence shows:\n1. Training loss barely decreases\n2. Validation accuracy shows high volatility with a net decline (0.8412 at epoch 1 \u2192 0.8266 at epoch 19)\n3. This is directly caused by the label_noise_fraction parameter in the configuration\n\n**Repair:**\nThe label noise fraction should be reduced to a more reasonable value. A typical default would be 0.05 (5% label noise) or 0.0 (no noise), depending on the intended robustness testing.",
